A Byzantine flavour submod for [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3737705389]Flavorium Universalis[/url].

[b]Requires the Fate of the Phoenix content pack.[/b] Subscribe to this alongside the base mod if you want the Greek path to pay, a Rome you govern without moving to, and the historical costs attached to the things that historically cost.

[hr][/hr]

[i]Early Alpha preview. The mod is not fully tested yet. The western province situation and exarchate content, along with the Indian side of the mission tree, are untested, and balance will change.[/i]


[h2]Why this exists[/h2]

Fate of the Phoenix puts every unique reward on the Latin side of the Latinitas and Rhomanismos slider, and the only route to the Roman tag force-moves your capital to Rome. A Greek-speaking Orthodox empire in 1337 is therefore pushed toward re-Latinising itself and abandoning Constantinople, which has no basis in the period.

This submod answers that: it makes the Greek path pay, builds a Rome you govern from Constantinople, and prices the reconquest at what a reconquest costs.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Taxis against Dynatoi[/h2]

The pack gives Byzantium one societal value, and it is an outward-facing question. Taxis against Dynatoi is the other one, and it decided more than the language question did.

[b]Taxis[/b] is order: the fisc, the bureaux, the salaried official, the crown. It buys bureaucratic efficiency, crown power, cabinet efficiency and tax, and costs you manpower and the goodwill of the nobility.

[b]Dynatoi[/b] are the powerful: provincial magnates, pronoia-holders, the families who supply the soldiers and expect to be consulted. They buy manpower, levies, cheaper subjects and noble satisfaction, and cost the treasury its ability to assess anything.

Neither is the correct answer. Crossing the two axes gives four distinct endgames: a westernised administrative empire, a Latin-feudal Romania, the restored bureaucratic autokratoria, and a magnate confederation with an emperor on top of it.

The Greek side also has something to unlock. A parallel advance line answers the Latin advances point for point, and the [b]tagmata[/b] give a heavy infantry line to set against the legionaries. Both are gated on the Rhomanismos side, so pushing Latin genuinely costs you access to them.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Rome, without moving to Rome[/h2]

Appoint an [b]exarch[/b]: a named character from your court, given a seat in the recovered west and a term of appointment, governing as your subject while your capital stays where the empire actually is.

There are four seats, one for each late-Roman praetorian prefecture: [b]Ravenna[/b] in Italy, [b]Carthage[/b] in Africa, [b]Spania[/b] on the Iberian coast, and [b]the Gauls[/b] on the Rhone. One exarch per region.

You do not need to know the feature exists to find it. Once enough of a region is in imperial hands, the officials administering it by dispatch write to say they are drowning, and the event nominates a man from your own council.

[list]
[*] [b]The friction runs across a reign.[/b] He makes a decision without asking and it is a good decision. His court adopts Latin usage, because he was sent to govern Rome. He corresponds with Venice and Genoa, because a governor who does not learns everything six months late.
[*] [b]The ending is accumulated, not chosen.[/b] Outcomes range from a hereditary exarchal dynasty that outlives its creator to a fully absorbed province run by salaried officials. A loyalty score decides it, and it has been moving since the appointment.
[*] [b]What he wants is tracked separately from how loyal he is.[/b] He can be perfectly loyal and still want the Romagna. The better answers to his requests are gated on your ruler or on your cabinet, so a charismatic negotiator at council can make a refusal land as policy rather than a snub.
[*] [b]Each province fails in its own way.[/b] Africa is a frontier that never settles. Spania is a coastal strip that cannot feed its own garrison. Gaul has been continuously Roman and does not regard itself as having been recovered.
[/list]

Holding Rome also means holding a quarrel with the papacy that has no resolution: which rite is celebrated, whether the Pope is a guest or a hostage, and an excommunication that costs an Orthodox emperor almost nothing and his Catholic-governing exarch a great deal.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]The West, and the arithmetic that turns on you[/h2]

Seat your first exarch and a situation opens. It tracks two things that are [b]not[/b] two ends of one bar: imperial grip is how firmly Constantinople governs the west, western autonomy is how much the west decides for itself. Both can be high at once, which is the historical interest of the arrangement.

The prefectures form a body with the emperor at its head. It begins dormant, and convening it is your decision alone. Votes are weighted: the Augustus 3, the Senior Exarch 2, each other exarch 1. With one prefecture you cannot lose. With two it is a tie and the motion fails. With three the west carries.

Nothing about that needs balancing. It turns against you because you succeeded, one province at a time, and the body that outvotes you is the one you summoned.

The consistory votes on the four powers a late-Roman prefecture actually exercised: who the western armies obey, where the revenue goes, what is celebrated in the western churches, and how a dead prefect is replaced. A west that votes itself local usage drifts Latin, and the drift lands on the empire rather than the provinces.

Seven repeatable actions run alongside it, available with or without mission packs. Order a census, pay the donative, demand the tribute, send a strategos, confirm an exarch's son, charter a commune, or summon an exarch home. Six are decisions. Whether he comes when called is not one, and the odds read off a loyalty score the other six have been moving for decades.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Also included[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]The church union[/b] and its consequences, handled as the political problem it was.
[*] [b]The Justinianic reconquest[/b] as a mission path, priced at what it cost.
[*] [b]A Mamluk confrontation[/b] in the Levant, which reads the map rather than assuming the 1337 start.
[*] [b]Eleven Byzantine bureaucracies[/b] extended with Taxis and Dynatoi drift that scales with funding.
[*] [b]French, German and Spanish[/b] localization throughout.
[/list]

